# merlint configuration (#153).
#
# Enforcing (#173): the curated rule set is at zero, so CI blocks on any finding.
# The excluded rules below conflict with deliberate project conventions; every
# other rule stays enabled. See MERLINT.md for the full rationale.
#
# Excluded rules and why:
#   E205 - we use Printf/Format throughout, not the Fmt module.
#   E320 - long, descriptive identifiers (e.g. fk_child_has_ref_multi_in_tx)
#          are intentional and aid readability over terse names.
#   E410 - our doc-comment shape differs from merlint's preferred odoc style.
#   E600/E605/E606/E610 - test-layout rules. Our suite is behavior-oriented
#          (a single test file exercises many library modules) and lives flat
#          under test/; it is not a 1:1 mirror of lib/ modules. These fire on
#          every test file (and E605 on lib modules) by design, not as defects.
#
#   E618 - "avoid X__Y module access". Every finding is the lwt_ppx-generated
#          binding `__ppx_lwt_0` from `let%lwt`/`let*` expansion, attributed back
#          to the source line. It is ppx output, not hand-written double-underscore
#          module access; there are zero genuine `Module__Sub` uses in the tree.
#          A pure tooling artifact, like the test-layout rules above.
#
#   E005 - long functions. Excluded (#171): adopting ocamlformat (profile
#          janestreet) makes the formatter own vertical layout — one list/record
#          item per line, ;; separators, blank lines between bindings — which
#          re-inflated line counts past E005's thresholds for 73 functions that
#          #168 had driven to zero under the old hand formatting. A line-count
#          linter and an opinionated auto-formatter both governing layout is
#          redundant: E005 would now measure formatting, not logic, and
#          hand-decomposing to satisfy it fights the formatter (a margin/profile
#          bump re-breaks it). The #168 structural decompositions remain in the
#          code; only the line-count metric is retired.
#
# Naming-convention opinions we decline (#153) - merlint's preferred terse
# style conflicts with this project's deliberate descriptive naming (same
# rationale as E320 above):
#   E331 - "redundant function prefixes": wants create_table->table,
#          find_index->index, get_fk_enforcement->fk_enforcement. Our create_*/
#          find_*/get_* names mirror the SQL DDL they implement and read clearer
#          than bare nouns.
#   E325 - "find_/get_ option semantics": wants find_table->get_table. This
#          directly CONTRADICTS E331 (which wants find_table->table) on the very
#          same definitions, so the two cannot both be satisfied; our find_*
#          (returns option) convention is deliberate.
#   E332 - "prefer 'v' constructor": wants Pager.create->Pager.v, Mem.create->
#          Mem.v, etc. We use `create` consistently across modules (same stance
#          as the E205 Fmt opinion).
#   E330 - "redundant module name". The genuinely-redundant cases were fixed in
#          code (Fts_query.fts_query type -> Fts_query.t; Store.store_of ->
#          txn_store). What remains is intentional domain naming: `page_size`
#          (Page/Wal/Mem/Unix_file/Mirage all expose `val page_size : int` as
#          part of the BLOCK device interface — it mirrors SQLite's own term and
#          renaming its ~140 cross-module references to `size` would both lose
#          clarity and diverge the backends), and `Wal.wal_magic` (the magic
#          constant; bare `magic` collides with the per-read local). Excluded
#          for these deliberate constants.
#
# Scoped to test/ only (#167, #173):
#   E105 - catch-all exception handlers. In tests these are idiomatic best-effort
#          cleanup (temp-file teardown `(try Unix.unlink p with _ -> ())`,
#          Fun.protect finalizers, env-var parse fallbacks); a swallowed error in
#          test scaffolding just skips cleanup. E105 stays ENABLED for lib/ and
#          bin/, where the 12 production sites were narrowed to precise exceptions
#          (Unix.Unix_error / Failure / Parser.Error / Invalid_argument).
#   E110 - silenced warnings (#173). In tests these are deliberate scaffolding:
#          `[@warning "-69"]` on mock record fields that are written but never
#          read (test_btree mock pagers), and `[@@warning "-32"]` on *_lwt helper
#          variants retained during the monadic-test migration (#161). E110 stays
#          ENABLED for lib/+bin/, where the 5 production sites were resolved by
#          dropping stale suppressions (the bindings were actually used) and
#          deleting two genuinely-dead functions.
#
# Multiple file blocks union (rule_config.should_exclude is List.exists), so the
# test/ block layers E105 on top of the global exclusions for test files.
#
# Patterns are unquoted: merlint's config parser does not strip surrounding
# quotes, so "**/*.ml*" would be taken literally.
#
# Scoped to jepsen/ (Jepsen verification harness, #177/#179):
#   E300 - "variant naming convention". The op/workload/nemesis variants
#          (SetAdd, SetRead, BankRead, NoNemesis, ...) deliberately mirror the
#          Jepsen/EDN workload protocol names rather than OCaml Snake_case, so
#          they read 1:1 against the Jepsen side. Same stance as the naming
#          opinions (E320/E331/E325/E330) declined above.
#   E505 - "missing .mli". These are internal harness modules (one per workload
#          / nemesis), not a public API surface; .mli files add ceremony without
#          encapsulation value here. Same spirit as the E600-series test-layout
#          exclusions above.

rules:
  - files: **/*.ml*
    exclude: [E205, E320, E410, E600, E605, E606, E610, E618, E331, E325, E332, E330, E005]
  - files: test/*.ml*
    exclude: [E105, E110]
  - files: jepsen/**/*.ml*
    exclude: [E300, E505]
